\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Hybrid Recurrent Architectures.}
The design space for combining attention with recurrence has expanded along two axes: where mixing occurs and how much attention is allocated. Jamba \citep{lieber2024jamba} interleaves Mamba and Transformer blocks across layers, while Griffin \citep{de2024griffin} and RecurrentGemma \citep{botev2024recurrentgemma} pair gated linear recurrences with local attention. RWKV-6 \citep{peng2024eagle} adds matrix-valued recurrent states alongside attention-like token mixing, and Hymba \citep{dong2024hymba} mixes attention and recurrence within individual heads rather than across layers. Mamba-2 \citep{dao2024mamba2} establishes a formal duality between structured state spaces and attention. Across these designs, comparative evaluation has relied on aggregate benchmark scores. \citet{waleffe2024empirical} conducted a large-scale comparison showing that Mamba-2 hybrids outperform pure Transformers and pure SSMs across standard benchmarks, further establishing hybrids as the dominant paradigm. \citet{basu2026routing} analyzed the representation requirements for content-based routing in hybrid models, showing that a single attention layer creates latent structure enabling selective token routing. \citet{wang2025hybrid} provide a controlled model zoo that holds training conditions constant while varying architecture and attention ratio, which enables direct comparison. We use this zoo to study behavioral effects of attention at both aggregate and per-example granularity.

\paragraph{Linear Recurrent Models.}
Linear recurrent models evolved from structured state spaces to selective and gated variants. S4 \citep{gu2022s4} introduced parameterized state-space layers with linear complexity for long-range dependencies, and \citet{katharopoulos2020transformers} established a theoretical bridge by showing that softmax attention can be approximated by linear dot-product kernels. Mamba \citep{gu2024mamba} extended S4 with input-dependent selection, which allows the recurrence to filter information based on content rather than position. Subsequent designs pursued different gating strategies: RetNet \citep{sun2023retnet} uses multi-scale exponential decay, HGRN2 \citep{qin2024hgrn2} applies gated linear recurrence with state expansion, and GatedDeltaNet \citep{yang2024gateddeltanet} combines gated updates with delta rules. These three architectures serve as the recurrent backbones in our study, and their distinct parameterizations make them a natural test bed for whether attention produces uniform or architecture-specific behavioral effects.

\paragraph{Underspecification and Model Agreement.}
\citet{damour2022underspecification} formalized underspecification: a training pipeline admits multiple models with equivalent aggregate performance but different per-prediction behavior. Their study documented this across computer vision, medical imaging, and NLP, showing that standard model selection does not resolve the ambiguity. \citet{mccoy2020berts} demonstrated a concrete NLP instance: BERT models trained from identical configurations but different random seeds achieve similar aggregate scores while diverging on which constructions each seed handles correctly. \citet{fort2019deep} found that different initializations converge to distinct loss landscape modes with different generalization profiles. \citet{kornblith2019similarity} proposed Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA) to measure representational similarity and showed that networks with different initializations learn partially different internal representations. These studies attribute per-prediction divergence to variation in initialization or training noise within a single architecture. Our work tests whether the same phenomenon arises when the source of variation is the recurrent backbone itself.

\paragraph{Ensemble Diversity.}
The relationship between prediction diversity and ensemble performance has a long theoretical history. \citet{krogh1994ensembles} proved that ensemble error decomposes into average individual error minus a diversity term, so disagreement among members directly reduces ensemble error. \citet{kuncheva2003measures} surveyed ten pairwise diversity measures and found that no single measure reliably predicts ensemble accuracy, which limits the practical use of diversity for ensemble selection. \citet{lakshminarayanan2017simple} showed that deep ensembles trained from different random initializations produce well-calibrated uncertainty estimates, and that diversity arises naturally from stochastic optimization without explicit diversity objectives. The hybrid architectures we study are not ensembles, but the per-example disagreement we observe between architectures parallels the diversity that ensemble theory predicts should reduce combined error.

\paragraph{Per-Example Analysis.}
A growing line of work treats evaluation examples as non-exchangeable, assigning per-example difficulty or informativeness scores. \citet{swayamdipta2020dataset} introduced dataset cartography, which maps each training example into a confidence-variability space and identifies easy, ambiguous, and hard regions of a dataset. \citet{rodriguez2021evaluation} applied Item Response Theory from psychometrics to NLP benchmarks, estimating per-item difficulty and discriminability that reveal how informative each example is about model quality. \citet{ethayarajh2022understanding} proposed V-usable information as a measure of dataset difficulty relative to a specific model family, formalizing the intuition that difficulty depends on the learner. These methods characterize examples through the lens of a single model or model family. Our helped/hurt decomposition is a cross-architecture variant: we classify each example by how adding attention changes each architecture's prediction, then measure whether architectures agree on which examples are affected.

\paragraph{Attention Interpretability.}
Whether attention weights carry interpretive signal has been debated. \citet{jain2019attention} argued that attention distributions do not reliably indicate input importance, since alternative weight configurations can produce identical outputs. \citet{wiegreffe2019attention} countered that alternative explanations do not invalidate attention-based ones under appropriate conditions. \citet{clark2019what} showed that specific BERT attention heads capture syntactic relations such as coreference, even if the full attention matrix is not a faithful global explanation. \citet{olsson2022induction} identified induction heads, attention heads that implement in-context copying by attending to prior occurrences of the current token's context. Our attention weight analysis is observational: we report differences in attention entropy and cosine similarity between helped and hurt examples but do not claim causal status for attention patterns.

\section{Method}\label{sec:method}

We describe the model configurations, evaluation benchmarks, and analytical framework used to measure how attention affects cross-architecture agreement at both aggregate and per-example levels.

\subsection{Model Zoo}\label{ssec:model_zoo}

We use the pretrained model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}, which provides controlled comparisons across architectures and attention ratios. The zoo contains three linear recurrent architectures: GatedDeltaNet \citep{yang2024gateddeltanet}, RetNet \citep{sun2023retnet}, and HGRN2 \citep{qin2024hgrn2}. These three architectures represent distinct approaches to linear recurrence: GatedDeltaNet uses gated delta rules, RetNet uses multi-scale exponential decay, and HGRN2 uses gated linear recurrence with state expansion. Each architecture is trained at five attention ratios: 0\% (pure recurrent), 4\%, 7.7\%, 14.3\%, and 25\%. We adopt the h$k$-1 notation from the original work, where one attention layer is interleaved per every $k{+}1$ total layers: h3-1 corresponds to 25\% attention, h6-1 to 14.3\%, h12-1 to 7.7\%, and h24-1 to 4\%. All configurations are available at two scales: 340M and 1.3B parameters. Within each scale, all models share identical training data, tokenizer, and hyperparameters; they differ only in architecture and attention configuration. This controlled design isolates the effect of attention from confounds such as training data or optimization differences. The zoo also includes a Transformer baseline (pure attention) at both scales.

\subsection{Task Taxonomy}\label{ssec:task_taxonomy}

We evaluate on 10 benchmarks grouped into four categories defined by the primary cognitive demand of each task. This taxonomy was pre-registered prior to analysis (see Appendix~A for pre-registration details), not derived post hoc from observed results. The categories test whether the effect of attention on cross-architecture variance depends on task type.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Retrieval}: LAMBADA \citep{paperno2016lambada}, which requires predicting a final word from long-range context without reasoning or world knowledge.
    \item \textbf{Comprehension}: BoolQ, a reading comprehension task with yes/no answers.
    \item \textbf{Reasoning}: HellaSwag, ARC-Challenge \citep{clark2018arc}, WinoGrande, and PIQA. COPA ($n = 100$) serves as a supplementary reasoning benchmark.
    \item \textbf{Mixed}: ARC-Easy, MMLU, SciQ, and XStoryCloze, which combine retrieval, comprehension, and reasoning demands.
\end{itemize}

All evaluations use the lm-evaluation-harness framework \citep{gao2024lmeval} with default prompts; accuracy serves as the primary metric for all benchmarks.

\subsection{Convergence Metrics}\label{ssec:convergence_metrics}

We quantify cross-architecture agreement as a function of attention ratio. For each benchmark and attention ratio, we compute the cross-architecture standard deviation $\sigma$ over the three architectures' accuracy scores. To measure the relationship between attention and convergence, we compute the Spearman rank correlation $\rho$ between attention ratio rank and $\sigma$ across the five attention configurations ($n = 5$). A negative $\rho$ indicates that higher attention ratios are associated with lower cross-architecture variance.

We also report the convergence ratio $\sigma_{\text{pure}} / \sigma_{\text{h3-1}}$, defined as the standard deviation under the pure-recurrent configuration divided by that under the highest attention ratio (25\%). Values greater than 1 indicate that attention reduces cross-architecture variance.

\subsection{Per-Example Decomposition}\label{ssec:per_example_decomposition}

To move beyond aggregate metrics, we decompose performance changes at the individual-example level on LAMBADA (5{,}153 examples). For each architecture, we compare the pure-recurrent baseline against its h3-1 hybrid and classify each example into one of three categories:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Helped}: correct in the hybrid but incorrect in the pure model.
    \item \textbf{Hurt}: correct in the pure model but incorrect in the hybrid.
    \item \textbf{Unchanged}: both models agree (both correct or both incorrect).
\end{itemize}

The net effect for each architecture is $|\text{helped}| - |\text{hurt}|$. A positive net effect means attention produces more correct predictions than it removes; a negative net effect means the opposite.

To measure cross-architecture overlap in per-example effects, we compute the pairwise Jaccard similarity $J = |A \cap B| / |A \cup B|$ between the helped sets of two architectures and, separately, between their hurt sets. $J = 1$ indicates identical sets; $J = 0$ indicates no overlap. Low $J$ values would indicate that different architectures are affected on different subsets of examples, which would constitute per-example divergence even when aggregate performance converges.

\subsection{Statistical Methods}\label{ssec:statistical_methods}

All statistical tests are two-tailed unless otherwise noted.

\paragraph{Permutation test.} With only $n = 5$ attention ratios, we use an exact permutation test for $\rho$. All $5! = 120$ permutations of the attention ratio ranks are enumerated, and the two-tailed $p$-value is the fraction of permutations yielding $|\rho| \geq |\rho_{\text{obs}}|$.

\paragraph{Bootstrap confidence intervals.} We construct 95\% BCa (bias-corrected and accelerated) bootstrap confidence intervals for $\rho$ using 10{,}000 resamples at the architecture level. When the BCa correction degenerates at a boundary value, we report percentile confidence intervals instead.

\paragraph{Multiple comparisons.} We apply Bonferroni correction across six pre-registered benchmarks (Appendix~A). The four remaining benchmarks are corrected separately across all 10 benchmarks and reported as exploratory.

\paragraph{Within- and cross-architecture tests.} For within-architecture comparisons of per-example properties (e.g., token count of helped vs.\ hurt examples), we use the Mann-Whitney U test. For cross-architecture comparisons of per-example effect distributions, we use the Kruskal-Wallis test. Both are rank-based nonparametric tests, chosen because the per-example effect distributions are non-normal and contain ties.

\paragraph{Attention weight analysis.} To probe whether attention mechanisms differ between helped and hurt examples, we extract attention weight matrices from each attention layer via forward hooks and compute two quantities: (1) cosine similarity between the mean attention distributions of two architectures at each layer, which measures cross-architecture agreement in attention allocation; and (2) Shannon entropy of the attention distribution for each example, which measures how concentrated or diffuse the attention pattern is. Details of the extraction procedure appear in Appendix~D.

